 "stem": "In the quadrilateral $ABCD$, $m(\\angle A) = (2x + {=a})^\\circ$ and $m(\\angle C) = (3x + {=c})^\\circ$. If $ABCD$ is a cyclic quadrilateral, find $m(\\angle C)$ in degrees.",
 "answer": "C",
 "solution": [
  "$\\angle A$ and $\\angle C$ are opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral, so they are supplementary.",
  "$(2x + {=a}) + (3x + {=c}) = 180$.",
  "$5x + {=a + c} = 180$, so $5x = {=180 - a - c}$ and $x = {=x0}$.",
  "$m(\\angle C) = 3({=x0}) + {=c} = {=C}^\\circ$.",
  "Check: $\\angle A = {=A}^\\circ$ and ${=A} + {=C} = 180$."
 ],
 "proposed_misconceptions": [],
 "notes": "Set up and solve the supplementary-angles equation, then substitute."
